\begin{document}
```

2. Add the `\tableofcontents` command at the beginning of the document, right after `\begin{document}`:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{tocloft} % for customizing the TOC

\begin{document}
  \tableofcontents
```

3. To add sections, subsections, and other levels to the TOC, use the standard LaTeX sectioning commands, such as `\section`, `\subsection`, and so on. Here's an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{tocloft} % for customizing the TOC

\begin{document}
  \tableofcontents

  \section{First Section}
    \subsection{First Subsection}
    \subsubsection{First Subsubsection}

  \section{Second Section}
    \subsection{Second Subsection}
    \subsubsection{Second Subsubsection}

\end{document}
```

4. LaTeX will automatically generate the TOC based on the sectioning commands you use in the document. You can customize the appearance of the TOC using the `tocloft` package options. For example, to change the font size of the TOC entries, you can add the following lines to your preamble:

```latex
\usepackage{tocloft}

\renewcommand{\cftsecfont}{\large} % change font size for section entries
\renewcommand{\cftsubsecfont}{\normalsize} % change font size for subsection entries
\renewcommand{\cftsubsubsecfont}{\small} % change font size for subsubsection entries
```

Now, when you compile your document, LaTeX will generate a TOC automatically based on the sectioning commands you used. Here's an example of what your document might look like:

```
